## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical implementation of order-statistics data structures with batched updates. It expects a solution that balances fast batch updates with O(1) median queries by maintaining the median explicitly.

## Key Concepts
- Two balanced structures (lower and upper halves) to partition the data
- Lazy/deferred deletions: mark elements as deleted rather than removing them immediately, deferring cleanup to future operations
- Amortized analysis for batch updates and overall complexity
- Trade-offs between simple heaps and BST-based structures

## Code Example
```python
import heapq

class MedianTracker:
    def __init__(self):
        self.low = []   # max-heap (negatives)
        self.high = []  # min-heap
        self.deleted = set()

    def _cleanup(self):
        while self.low and -self.low[0] in self.deleted:
            self.deleted.remove(-heapq.heappop(self.low))
        while self.high and self.high[0] in self.deleted:
            self.deleted.remove(heapq.heappop(self.high))

    def insert(self, x):
        if not self.low or x <= -self.low[0]:
            heapq.heappush(self.low, -x)
        else:
            heapq.heappush(self.high, x)
        self._rebalance()
        self._cleanup()

    def delete(self, x):
        self.deleted.add(x)
        self._cleanup()

    def _rebalance(self):
        if len(self.low) < len(self.high):
            heapq.heappush(self.low, -heapq.heappop(self.high))
        elif len(self.low) - len(self.high) > 1:
            heapq.heappush(self.high, -heapq.heappop(self.low))

    def median_at_least(self, T):
        self._cleanup()
        return -self.low[0] >= T if self.low else False
```

## Why This Is Asked
Maintaining the number of distinct substrings under incremental appends tests knowledge of suffix automata, amortized analysis, and practical update strategies. It highlights how to avoid recomputation and reason about state growth.

## Key Concepts
- Suffix Automaton (SAM) for distinct substrings
- Online extensions and delta = len(cur) - len(link(cur))
- Amortized analysis over batches
- Space linear in string length

## Code Example
```javascript
class SAMNode {
  constructor() {
    this.next = {};
    this.link = -1;
    this.len = 0;
  }
}
class SuffixAutomaton {
  constructor() {
    this.st = [new SAMNode()];
    this.last = 0;
    this.size = 1;
  }
  extend(c){
    const st = this.st; const cur = this.size++;
    st.push(new SAMNode()); st[cur].len = st[this.last].len + 1;
    let p = this.last;
    while (p >= 0 && !(c in st[p].next)) { st[p].next[c] = cur; p = st[p].link; }
    if (p === -1) { st[cur].link = 0; }
    else {
      const q = st[p].next[c];
      if (st[p].len + 1 === st[q].len) {
        st[cur].link = q;
      } else {
        const clone = this.size++;
        st.push(Object.assign(new SAMNode(), st[q]));
        st[clone].len = st[p].len + 1;
        while (p >= 0 && st[p].next[c] === q) { st[p].next[c] = clone; p = st[p].link; }
        st[q].link = st[cur].link = clone;
      }
    }
    this.last = cur;
  }
}
```

